326: ## SC.4. The extending normal spinor and actual Hopf circle line
327: 
328: The finite-base source has a nowhere-zero holomorphic two-form. In the
329: coordinates (SC.1), its residue expression is a nonzero holomorphic
330: unit times \(dX\wedge dy\). Together with \(d\rho\) it trivializes the
331: ambient complex determinant along the normalization. The complex normal
332: line of the immersion (SC.2) on \(W\) is consequently
333: \[
334:  L=N_f\cong\mathcal O(-D_+-D_-).                     \tag{SC.12}
335: \]
336: This is a determinant isomorphism, not merely a degree equality. In the
337: finite coordinate chart its normal covector is the explicit cross product
338: \[
339:  (3s^2-\rho^2)\,dX-2s\,dy
340:                     +2\rho(s^2-\rho^2)\,d\rho.     \tag{SC.13}
341: \]
342: It is nonzero everywhere on \(W\) in this chart. Changing to the
343: infinite normalization coordinate gives the \(\mathcal O(-2)\)
344: normal transition. The divisor identity
345: \(\operatorname{div}(s^2-\rho^2)=D_++D_--2\{s=\infty\}\)
346: gives the displayed actual isomorphism (SC.12).
347: 
348: The normal bundle of the real graph is isomorphic to
349: \(L_{\mathbb R}\oplus\mathbb R_\gamma\): quotient by its tangent
350: graph gives the identity on the source normal two-plane and the gamma
351: coordinate, with only a triangular shear. Its linear contraction is
352: the precise graph-normal comparison. Retain the same constant orientation
353: change as in the positive-Hopf convention of K7.
354: 
355: Define the rank-two complex spinor bundle
356: \[
357:  E=(\mathcal O\oplus L)\otimes\mathcal O(D_+)
358:        =\mathcal O(D_+)\oplus\mathcal O(-D_-).        \tag{SC.14}
359: \]
360: Its determinant is \(\mathcal O(D_+-D_-)\), trivialized by the literal
361: Cartier ratio \((s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\). Thus \(E\) is an actual
362: \(SU(2)\) bundle, and
363: \[
364:  \operatorname{ad}E\cong
365:               \mathbb R\oplus L_{\mathbb R}.        \tag{SC.15}
366: \]
367: The off-diagonal summand is exactly
368: \(\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal O(D_+),\mathcal O(-D_-))=L\).
369: Its Hopf sphere bundle carries the meridian line with determinant
370: class \(b=0\).
371: 
372: This is the actual outer circle line. In the normalization coordinate
373: \(Z=(s-\rho)/(s+\rho)\), the two Cox normal coordinates have transition
374: \(c_i'=Zc_i\) and physical weights \((-1,+1)\). Conjugating the
375: negative-weight coordinate gives the unitary transition
376: \[
377:        \operatorname{diag}(\overline Z/|Z|,Z/|Z|),
378: \]
379: which is precisely that of (SC.14), after the explicit Hermitian
380: identification of the conjugate tautological line with its dual. It
381: intertwines the physical circle actions. The quaternion formula
382: \[
383:  q_{\rm H}=(1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})
384: \]
385: is the same formula as K30; its second-chart expression gives the
386: identical vector. The ratio \([s-\rho:s+\rho]\) is a genuine map from
387: \(W\) to \(\mathbf P^1\), since its only common zero was removed.
388: Consequently this Cox/Hopf identification extends over all of \(W\),
389: including the inner link, with the original ordered branch marking.
390: 
391: The pullback of the source Picard line to
392: \(\mathbf P^1\times D_\rho\) has degree zero on every normalization
393: fibre and hence is a line from the parameter disc. Rigidifying it along
394: the zero section gives a literal trivialization. The source-line unit
395: in the suspension isotopy above is retained in this trivialization.
396: It contributes the same extending scalar gauge on both sides of
397: (SC.14). No degree-zero line or scalar winding is discarded.
398: 
399: The circle line also extends on the whole excision complement; this
400: does not follow just from constructing its restriction to the normal
401: sphere bundle. Put \(Z'= (Y\times D_\rho)\setminus\operatorname{int}B^7\).
402: It has \(H^2(Z';\mathbb Z)=H^3(Z';\mathbb Z)=0\). The codimension-three
403: Thom sequence of the properly embedded connected \(W\) therefore gives
404: \[
405:  H^2(Z'\setminus W;\mathbb Z)\xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }
406:              H^0(W;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z.             \tag{SC.15a}
407: \]
408: The isomorphism is evaluation on the oriented normal meridian. Choose
409: the line with meridian value one. On the outer complement the actual
410: Euler line has this value, and \(H^2\) of that complement is also
411: \(\mathbb Z\): each fibre is the complement of the unknotted \(S^2\)
412: in \(S^5\), with \(H^1=0,H^2=\mathbb Z\), and the oriented return
413: preserves its generator. The Wang sequence has no other degree-two
414: term. The ratio of the two lines is therefore topologically trivial;
415: multiplication by a chosen nonzero section of that ratio is the actual
416: boundary bundle isomorphism. It retains the physical circle action.
417: 
418: Furthermore restriction
419: \(H^2(S(\nu W))\to H^2(S(\nu W)|_{F\times S^1})\) is an isomorphism:
420: it is an isomorphism both on the base \(H^2\) and on the meridian
421: summand of the sphere-bundle sequence. Thus the restriction of the line
422: in (SC.15a) is exactly the line (SC.14), whose outer identification was
423: just given in Cox coordinates. In particular its determinant class on
424: \(W\) is zero. On the inner link it is the same oriented meridian line.
425: Possible boundary isomorphisms differ by circle-valued gauges; their
426: outer parameter winding is extended over the specified
427: \(D^3\times S^1\) cap below. This retains that winding rather than
428: assuming it vanishes in the excision complement.
429: 
